  "response": "`crimes context src/billing.ts --root .` — **risk: high** (3 high / 6 medium / 2 low, 11 findings), `analysis_status: complete`, `test_gap` bottom-quartile, likely test `src/billing.test.ts`.\n\n## Top 3 changes\n\n### 1. Carve `generateInvoice` into named helpers — and delete the duplicated rules it hides\n*Findings: `large_function/too_long` (high, sev 0.90, conf 0.95) · `large_file` (medium)*\n\n204 lines, 3.4× the 60-line domain threshold, plus the file at 310 lines. Length alone isn't the argument — what length is hiding is:\n\n- **Tax rates exist twice.** `billing.ts:145-155` inside the function and `tax()` at `billing.ts:257-266` carry the same seven-country ladder (`DE = 0.19` at both L149 and L261). A rate change has to land in two places and nothing makes that visible.\n- **Day math exists twice.** `const daysSince = …` is identical at L92 and L273 (`recommendUpgrade`), feeding two different policies.\n\nSeams to extract: `priceLines` → `loyaltyDiscount` → `applyPromo` → `scoreRisk` → `resolveTaxAndCurrency` → `renderInvoiceEmail` → `sendWithRetry`, leaving a thin orchestrator. Each is pure except the last two, so each becomes testable in isolation and `tax()` becomes the single authority.\n\n**Do this behind characterization tests first.** `test_gap` for this file is 0 in the report only because a test *file* exists — `src/billing.test.ts` asserts nothing real (the scan's `weak_test_signal` findings: one test with 0 assertions, one with only `toBeTruthy()`, and both call local stubs rather than `generateInvoice`). There is currently no net under this refactor.\n\n### 2. Fix `calculateRefundPreview` — the name lies and the authorization rule is prose only\n*Findings: `name_behavior_mismatch` (medium, conf 0.80) · `logic_in_comments` (medium, conf 0.74) — adjacent lines, one root cause*\n\n```ts\n// Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves.   // L39\nexport async function calculateRefundPreview(user: User, invoice: Invoice) {  // L40\n  await saveRefundAudit(user.id, invoice.id);\n  await sendRefundEmail(user.email, invoice.id);\n  return invoice.totalCents;\n}\n```\n\nA `calculate*Preview` name promises a pure read; the body writes an audit record and emails the customer. Any caller — human or agent — reasonably calls this to display a number and instead fires irreversible side effects. And the only statement of the owner/enterprise/support authorization rule is the comment above it: the body checks neither `user` role nor `invoice` plan. The detector's evidence is exact — \"domain terms not found nearby: owner, plan, billing, refund.\"\n\nSplit it: a pure `refundAmountCents(invoice)` for previews, and an `issueRefund(actor, invoice)` that starts with a real guard encoding the L39 rule (and a test for the denial path). This is the highest actual-consequence item in the file even though it scores below the `large_function` finding.\n\n### 3. Inject a clock — the report understates this one\n*Finding: `direct_date/clock_read` (medium, conf 0.90) — 7 reads at L57, 63, 92, 198, 238, 250, 273*\n\nThe evidence line says \"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading.\" That's conservative and, for L92, wrong in effect: `daysSince` derived from `Date.now()` at L92 decides the loyalty discount tier (`> 365` / `> 180` / `> 90`, L94-100) and adds 35 to the fraud `riskScore` when `< 1` (L134), which in turn sets `riskDecision: block | review | allow`. The same pattern at L273 decides every `recommendUpgrade` return value. So the clock is deciding **what customers are charged and whether an invoice is blocked**, not just what gets stamped into metadata.\n\nPass `now: number` (or a `Clock`) into `generateInvoice` / `recommendUpgrade` and thread it to the extracted helpers from #1. Cheap, and it's the precondition for writing the discount-tier and risk-score tests that don't exist today.\n\n## Deliberately not in the top 3\n\n- **The two `contract_drift` highs** (sev 0.90 / 0.96) outrank everything above on score, but they pair `billing.ts` against `src/api/state.ts` — a file whose own header says \"fixture material — do not import it from real code,\" with no import edge and `blast_radius: 0`. Highest severity, lowest actionability here; I'd confirm the relationship before touching either declaration rather than unifying two things that were never one contract.\n- **`swallowed_error` at L245-247** — `catch {}` with no binding inside a retry loop with no backoff; `metadata.emailAttempts` reports a number the caller can't distinguish from success. Real, small, fold it into the `sendWithRetry` extraction in #1.\n- **`commented_out_code` at L33-37** — note the scan-level warning: the suppression for this is *superseded* (`matches_current_finding: false`), so the \"keep for one more release\" decision has lapsed and the finding is live again with nothing silencing it. Either re-pin deliberately or delete the corpse.\n\nNo files changed — this was an analysis pass on an eval fixture that's meant to stay messy.",
